---
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import InfoPage from "@components/InfoPage.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How Rankings Work | NepalRanks"
  description="How NepalRanks positions are computed: net score from upvotes minus downvotes, rolling 24-hour vote allowances, comments attached to entries rather than positions, and the limits of administrator power."
>
  <InfoPage eyebrow="Mechanics">
    <h1>How rankings work</h1>
    <p>
      Every ranking on NepalRanks is a list of entries whose order is decided
      entirely by public voting. The rules below are the same for every category.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Each entry has a net score</h2>
    <p>
      An entry's score is <strong>upvotes minus downvotes</strong>. The ranking page
      sorts entries by that score, highest first, and the sorted position is the
      ranking. There is no weighting by recency, no editorial tie-break and no
      paid placement, positions are pure arithmetic on community votes.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Comments belong to entries, not to positions</h2>
    <p>
      Discussion is attached to the entry itself. When <em>Nabil Bank</em> moves from
      #4 to #1, its comments and comment scores move with it, because they describe
      the entry, not the number beside it. The same entry may appear in several
      rankings; each appearance is a separate discussion.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Vote allowances keep results honest</h2>
    <p>
      Each voter may cast <strong>one vote per ranking, up to 3 votes across the whole
      site per rolling 24-hour window</strong>, with only one vote per entry (switching your vote
      within the window replaces the earlier one). Full details live on the
      <a href="voting-rules/">voting rules</a> page.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Comments are scored too</h2>
    <p>
      Comment order on an entry's discussion is its own community vote:
      <strong>comment score = comment upvotes minus comment downvotes</strong>. Ranking
      previews show each entry's three highest-scoring comments.
    </p>

    <h2>5. What administrators can and cannot do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Can:</strong> create and retire rankings, add entries, moderate comments, remove abusive votes (score corrections only).</li>
      <li><strong>Cannot:</strong> reorder a ranking by hand or pin an entry to a position, positions always follow the vote scores.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. Transparency</h2>
    <p>
      Every page shows raw upvote, downvote and net-score numbers next to each entry,
      so you can always verify the ordering yourself.
    </p>
  </InfoPage>
</BaseLayout>
